WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome. In this class, we are going to
replace a

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
few misleading shortcuts with a practical
mental model for language

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
models. By the end, you should be able to

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
separate training from inference, describe
how text is generated step

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
by step, identify the parts of a larger AI

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
system that shape an answer, and explain
why confident wording

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
is not evidence. You do not need
mathematics or programming

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
experience. You only need to notice which
part of the

9
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
system is doing which job.

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.333
Start with the difference between training
and inference. During training,

11
00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:57.667
a model processes many examples and
adjusts numerical parameters so

12
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:04.000
that it becomes better at predicting
patterns. Those parameters are

13
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.333
not a tidy library of copied pages. They
are learned

14
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:16.667
behavior compressed into a very large set
of numbers. That

15
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:23.000
distinction matters because a trained
model can generalize beyond its

16
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.333
examples, but it can also fail, reproduce
undesirable patterns, or

17
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:35.667
produce a claim that was never present in
a source.

18
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:42.000
Inference is what happens when you use the
trained model.

19
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.333
Your current instructions and context are
processed with the learned

20
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:54.667
parameters to produce a response. A
product may surround the

21
00:01:54.667 --> 00:02:01.000
model with search, retrieval, files,
memory, or tools. Those features

22
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:07.333
can provide current evidence or perform
actions, but they are

23
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:13.667
separate system capabilities. Their
presence must be established and their

24
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:20.000
results must still be checked.

25
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.909
Here is a quick diagnostic. Imagine an
assistant gives you

26
00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:31.818
yesterday's weather without using a
weather tool or a supplied

27
00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:37.727
report. Do not say that inference has
searched a live

28
00:02:37.727 --> 00:02:43.636
database unless the product actually
provides that capability. The base

29
00:02:43.636 --> 00:02:49.545
model is applying learned behavior to the
current context. Now

30
00:02:49.545 --> 00:02:55.455
imagine the same assistant calls a weather
service and returns

31
00:02:55.455 --> 00:03:01.364
a timestamped result. The trained model
may decide how to

32
00:03:01.364 --> 00:03:07.273
ask for the data and how to explain it,

33
00:03:07.273 --> 00:03:13.182
while the tool supplies the current
observation. The answer is

34
00:03:13.182 --> 00:03:19.091
a system result, not evidence that the
model's training contained

35
00:03:19.091 --> 00:03:25.000
yesterday's weather.

36
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:31.333
Next, look at generation. Text is
represented as tokens, which

37
00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:37.667
may be whole words, pieces of words,
punctuation, or other

38
00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:44.000
symbols. Given the conversation so far, a
language model estimates

39
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.333
possible next tokens. Its decoding process
selects one, that token

40
00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:56.667
becomes part of the context, and the model
repeats the

41
00:03:56.667 --> 00:04:03.000
process. This is more useful than saying
the system looks

42
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.333
up a stored sentence. Learned patterns can
support coherent explanations,

43
00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:15.667
plans, code, and stories that were not
copied as complete

44
00:04:15.667 --> 00:04:22.000
answers. The same process can also
continue a plausible but

45
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.333
unsupported direction. Suppose the
unfinished sentence is, The capital of

46
00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:34.667
France is. A likely continuation is Paris.
That does not

47
00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:41.000
mean every likely continuation in every
domain is true. Generation

48
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.333
rewards a continuation that fits learned
patterns and current context.

49
00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:53.667
Verification is the separate job of
establishing whether the resulting

50
00:04:53.667 --> 00:05:00.000
claim is correct for your purpose.

51
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.667
Checkpoint: explain in one sentence why a
plausible continuation can

52
00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:13.333
still be factually wrong. Use the words
generation and verification

53
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:20.000
in your answer. Pause the class while you
answer.

54
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.000
A strong answer separates two jobs:
generation produces a continuation

55
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.000
that fits learned patterns and the
supplied context; verification tests

56
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.000
whether the claim is supported and
correct. If your answer

57
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.000
treated fluent wording as proof, revise it
now. Fluency can

58
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.000
make a statement easy to read, but it does

59
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.000
not provide a source, a test result, or a

60
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.000
calibrated confidence measure.

61
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:31.538
The model is only one part of the result

62
00:07:31.538 --> 00:07:38.077
you see. Instructions define the task and
boundaries. Context supplies

63
00:07:38.077 --> 00:07:44.615
facts, examples, files, prior messages,
and tool results. The product

64
00:07:44.615 --> 00:07:51.154
can add policies, retrieval, tool access,
output validation, and a

65
00:07:51.154 --> 00:07:57.692
particular model or decoding
configuration. When an answer is weak,

66
00:07:57.692 --> 00:08:04.231
diagnose the system before rewriting the
same prompt repeatedly. Missing

67
00:08:04.231 --> 00:08:10.769
facts may require authoritative context or
retrieval. A calculation may

68
00:08:10.769 --> 00:08:17.308
require a suitable tool and a checked
result. Conflicting instructions

69
00:08:17.308 --> 00:08:23.846
may require a clear authority order. A
high-impact decision may

70
00:08:23.846 --> 00:08:30.385
require a human owner even when the draft
is excellent.

71
00:08:30.385 --> 00:08:36.923
Model choice matters, but changing the
model is not a

72
00:08:36.923 --> 00:08:43.462
substitute for fixing a missing evidence
source or an unsafe

73
00:08:43.462 --> 00:08:50.000
workflow.

74
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.000
Try a diagnosis. An assistant is asked to
summarize a

75
00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:00.000
policy change, but the new policy was
never supplied and

76
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.000
the product has no retrieval tool. Choose
the missing element:

77
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:10.000
clearer tone, more evidence, faster
generation, or stronger confidence. Then

78
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.000
name the safest next step.

79
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:21.667
Finally, separate capability, style, and
correctness. A capable model can

80
00:11:21.667 --> 00:11:28.333
produce clear natural language across many
tasks. That capability does

81
00:11:28.333 --> 00:11:35.000
not turn confident tone into a
probability. The sentence I

82
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:41.667
am certain may itself be generated because
that style fits

83
00:11:41.667 --> 00:11:48.333
the context. For factual work, ask which
claims matter, identify

84
00:11:48.333 --> 00:11:55.000
authoritative sources, compare the claims
with those sources, run relevant

85
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:01.667
tests, and record unresolved uncertainty.
For creative work, evaluate usefulness,

86
00:12:01.667 --> 00:12:08.333
originality, audience fit, and constraints
instead of pretending there is

87
00:12:08.333 --> 00:12:15.000
one factual answer. A safe workflow
matches the verification method

88
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:21.667
to the consequence of being wrong. A
casual brainstorming idea

89
00:12:21.667 --> 00:12:28.333
may need lightweight review. Medical,
legal, financial, security, or operational

90
00:12:28.333 --> 00:12:35.000
decisions require qualified evidence and
accountable human judgment.

91
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:40.000
You now have the four-part mental model:
training creates learned

92
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:45.000
behavior; inference uses it; generation
proceeds step by step; and

93
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:50.000
the surrounding system plus verification
determines whether an output is

94
00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:55.000
useful and trustworthy. Next, compare two
outputs from the same

95
00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:00.000
low-risk task. Label each factual claim as
supported, unsupported, or

96
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.000
needing verification. Also record which
weaknesses can be fixed with

97
00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.000
better context and which require a tool,
source, test, or

98
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.000
human expert.

99
00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:30.000
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

100
00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:35.000
distinguish training from inference,
identify token-by-token generation,

101
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:40.000
recognize retrieval and tools as
surrounding capabilities, and choose

102
00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:45.000
evidence over confident tone. You can
review the transcript or

103
00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:50.000
return to any section before submitting.

104
00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Remember the shortest version: a language
model generates; a trustworthy

105
00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:00.000
workflow verifies. Keep those jobs
separate, and you will diagnose

106
00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.000
AI results more accurately.
